[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     scanf("%d", &y);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     driver(x, y);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There are no `if` statements, no early `return`s, and no explicit error paths.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every behavioural branch lives inside `scanf("%d", …)` and in the C integer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m semantics of `|`, `~` and the `int` store. The return values of both `scanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m calls are discarded, so a failed conversion is silent: the corresponding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m variable keeps its initialiser `0`, and the process still exits `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Consequences that tests must pin down:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - On total input failure both variables stay `0`, so the output is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0 | ~0 == -1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Whenever `y` ends up `0` (including every second-conversion failure) the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   output is `x | -1 == -1` regardless of `x`. Cases that are meant to exercise
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x` parsing therefore **must** supply a non-zero `y`, otherwise they pass
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   vacuously. The test suite does this deliberately in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `long_range_saturation` and `out_of_int_range_truncates`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
