[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where a naive translation diverges. Each is covered by a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m named test; all matched the C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`scanf` skips whitespace across newlines.** `%d` consumes any run of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `isspace` bytes (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`) before converting, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"5\n7"`, `"5\r\n7"` and `"\v5\f7"` all parse two integers. A `fgets` or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    line-oriented reader would only see one. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `two_items_separators`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Failure leaves the variable untouched, and exit status stays 0.** A Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    translation that treats a parse error as a fatal error (`expect`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `process::exit(1)`, writing to stderr) would diverge on stdout, stderr and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the exit code simultaneously. Covered by `first_conversion_fails`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `second_conversion_fails`, `no_input_at_all`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **Partial match leaves the offending byte queued for the next `scanf`.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"0x10 5"` converts `x = 0`, stops at `x`, and then the second `scanf` fails
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    on that same `x`, so `y` stays `0` (output `-1`, not `16` and not `5`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Likewise `"1,2"`, `"1.5 2.5"`, `"1e5 2"`, `"12abc 34"`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `partial_matches_and_queued_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **A lone sign is a matching failure, but glibc still consumes it.** `"-"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"+"`, `"--5"`, `"-+5"` all fail conversion. `"- 5"` fails the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    conversion at the space and then succeeds on `5` for `y`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `first_conversion_fails` and `signs_and_leading_zeros`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **Out-of-`int` values truncate rather than saturate.** glibc converts the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    digit string with `strtol` (64-bit `long` here) and the `%d` store narrows
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    to `int`, so `"1234 2147483648"` yields `y == -2147483648` and prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `2147483647`. Covered by `out_of_int_range_truncates`. Rust's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `str::parse::<i32>()` would reject these inputs outright — the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    correctly reproduces the C by converting to `i64` and casting with `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Out-of-`long` values saturate at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    *saturated* value is then narrowed.** `"5 99999999999999999999"` saturates
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `y` to `0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, narrows to `-1`, so `~y == 0` and the output is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `5`. The negative form `"-99999999999999999999 3"` saturates to `LONG_MIN`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    narrows to `0`, and prints `0 | ~3 == -4`. A translation that saturated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    directly in `i32`, or that wrapped the full big-integer value, gets both of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    these wrong. Covered by `long_range_saturation`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **`printf("%d", …)` then `puts("")`.** Exactly one newline, no space, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    leading text. A `println!("{}", result)` happens to match; a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `println!("result = {}", …)` or an extra flush-newline would not. Covered
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    everywhere, byte for byte.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Buffered reading must not lose tokens at a refill boundary.** The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    reader refills a 4,096-byte buffer, so a digit run or sign that straddles
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    byte 4,096 is a real risk. Covered by `buffer_boundary_cases` (digit run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    spanning 4,095 zeros, 4,096 newlines of leading whitespace, a sign landing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    on the boundary, 10,000-digit tokens, a 100,000-byte stream).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **Bytes that are not C whitespace must not be skipped.** UTF-8 NBSP
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`\xc2\xa0`), `\x7f`, NUL and bytes ≥ `0x80` are all conversion-stopping,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    not whitespace. Covered by `binary_and_non_ascii_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 10. **`main()` takes no parameters, so `argv` cannot change anything.** A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     translation that adds `--help` or positional-argument handling would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     diverge. Covered by `argv_is_ignored`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
